2026-06-30 14:56:59,363 - openai._base_client - DEBUG - Request options: {'method': 'post', 'url': '/chat/completions', 'timeout': 300, 'files': None, 'idempotency_key': 'stainless-python-retry-e5517443-938d-4283-b7af-4f460598ea77', 'security': {'bearer_auth': True}, 'content': None, 'json_data': {'messages': [{'role': 'system', 'content': 'You are an expert SDRF-Proteomics annotator for crosslinking mass spectrometry (XL-MS) datasets.\n\nExtract the following SDRF metadata terms related to the crosslinking reagent and reaction chemistry.\n\nTERMS TO EXTRACT:\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nterm: cross-linker\nsdrf_column: comment[cross-linker]\nontology: XLMOD (e.g. XLMOD:00004 for DSS/BS3)\ndescription: The chemical crosslinking reagent used. This is the primary crosslinker\n  identifier and must be formatted as a structured key-value string.\noutput_format: NT=<name>;AC=<XLMOD or ChEBI accession>;CL=<yes/no>;TA=<residue targets>\n  where:\n    NT = full chemical name or common abbreviation (e.g. BS3, DSSO, DSS, DSBU, EDC,\n         formaldehyde, SDA, sulfo-SDA, BMOE, Sulfo-LC-SDA, PIR, CBDPS)\n    AC = XLMOD or ChEBI accession if identifiable (else null)\n    CL = yes if MS-cleavable (DSSO, DSBU, DSBSO, DHSO, BMSO, SDASO, SSD, CBDPS, iQlnk, etc.), no if non-cleavable (BS3, DSS, EDC, PhoX, SDA, PIR, etc.), null if not stated\n    TA = comma-separated reactive residues (e.g. K,nterm or K,S,T,Y or D,E or C)\ninstructions: Extract from Methods section. If multiple crosslinkers, return a list.\n  ALWAYS populate NT with the reagent name as written in the text, even if the XLMOD\n  accession is unknown — set AC=null in that case. Only return value=null if no\n  crosslinker is mentioned anywhere in the provided text.\n  Common cleavable: DSSO (K,S,T,Y; AC=XLMOD:02126), DSBU (K,S,T,Y; AC=XLMOD:02120),\n    DSBSO (K,nterm; AC=null), Azide-A-DSBSO (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02222), Alkyne-A-DSBSO (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02223),\n    DSAU (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:09317), DSPU (K,nterm), DSSBU (K,nterm),\n    DHSO (carbonyl-reactive D,E; AC=XLMOD:02226), BMSO (cysteine-specific; C),\n    SDASO (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable), SDASO-L (K,nterm; photo+MS-cleavable),\n    SSD (K,nterm), CBDPS (K,nterm), iQlnk (K,nterm)\n  PIR-type enrichable (reporter-ion, NOT backbone MS-cleavable): PIR (K,nterm), BDP-NHP (K,nterm)\n  Common non-cleavable: BS3/BS2G (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02000/02005), DSS (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02001), EDC (D,E,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02010), formaldehyde (K,nterm)\n  IMAC-enrichable (non-cleavable): PhoX (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02258), pDSPE (K,nterm; AC=null)\n  Photo-crosslinkers (non-cleavable, UV-activated): diazirine (K or incorporated aa), benzophenone (Bpa, pBpa; C or incorporated aa), SDA/sulfo-SDA (K,nterm; AC=XLMOD:02134/02136), aryl azide\n  NOTE: Do NOT extract proximity labeling enzymes (TurboID, BioID, APEX, biotin-phenol+H2O2) as crosslinkers — these are NOT chemical cross-linking reagents.\n\nterm: crosslinker concentration\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker concentration]\nontology: PRIDE:0000951\ndescription: Concentration of the crosslinking reagent used in the reaction.\npattern: number + mM or uM (e.g. "1 mM", "500 uM", "0.5 mM")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: crosslinker to protein ratio\nsdrf_column: comment[crosslinker to protein ratio]\nontology: PRIDE:0000952\ndescription: Molar ratio of crosslinker to protein in the reaction.\npattern: ratio expression (e.g. "50:1", "100-fold molar excess", "5 mM per 1 mg/mL")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: quenching reagent\nsdrf_column: comment[quenching reagent]\nontology: PRIDE:0000953\ndescription: Reagent used to stop, quench, neutralize, terminate, or consume the crosslinking reaction.\ninstructions:\n  Extract only if explicitly stated in the paper, PRIDE metadata, or cited protocol text.\n  Search for synonyms including: quenched, stopped, terminated, neutralized, blocked,\n  residual crosslinker, washed with, incubated with, reaction was halted.\nCommon quenchers:\n  - NHS ester crosslinkers such as BS3, DSS, DSSO, DSBU: Tris, ammonium bicarbonate, glycine, lysine\n  - Formaldehyde crosslinking: glycine or Tris are common, but only annotate if explicitly stated\nFor formaldehyde datasets:\n  - Inspect the 3–5 sentences after the formaldehyde crosslinking sentence.\n  - If the text says "as described previously" or "as described before", and no quencher is named, attempt to read the cited artical and extract the quenching reagent from its methods section.\n    If that fall back failes then return value=null with confidence="low" and reasoning="quenching reagent not explicitly stated in provided text; may require cited protocol."\n  - Do NOT infer glycine solely because formaldehyde was used.\n  - Do NOT treat PBS washes alone as a quenching reagent.\n  - Treat Tris-containing lysis buffer as a possible quencher only if the text clearly implies it was use\n  \nterm: crosslink distance\nsdrf_column: characteristics[crosslink distance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000948\ndescription: Maximum Cα–Cα distance constraint (spacer arm length) provided by the crosslinker.\npattern: number + Å (e.g. "11.4 Å", "30 Å", "~27 Å")\ninstructions: Extract the stated spacer length or max distance. Common values:\n  BS3/DSS ≈ 11.4 Å, DSSO ≈ 10.1 Å, DSBU ≈ 12.5 Å, EDC ≈ 0 Å (zero-length).\n  Return null if not explicitly stated in the text.\n\nterm: chemical cross-linking coupled with ms\nsdrf_column: comment[chemical cross-linking coupled with ms]\nontology: PRIDE:0000430\ndescription: MS-based cross-linking methodology identifier.\nallowed_values: cross-linking mass spectrometry\ninstructions: Always return "cross-linking mass spectrometry" for any XL-MS dataset.\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null). For comment[cross-linker] the\n           "value" field should contain the full NT=...;AC=...;CL=...;TA=... string,\n           and also include separate sub-fields: "NT", "AC", "CL", "TA".\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null\n\nExample for comment[cross-linker]:\n{\n  "comment[cross-linker]": {\n    "value": "NT=DSSO;AC=XLMOD:02056;CL=yes;TA=K,S,T,Y",\n    "NT": "DSSO", "AC": "XLMOD:02056", "CL": "yes", "TA": "K,S,T,Y",\n    "confidence": "high", "evidence_quote": "crosslinked with 1 mM DSSO",\n    "evidence_location": "Methods"\n  }\n}'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nComprehensive identification of crosslinked peptides from methyltransferase Hmt1 and its substrate Npl3: use of multiple crosslinkers, mass spectrometry approaches and software platforms\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nChemical-Crosslinking Mass Spectrometry (XLMS) using MS-cleavable crosslinkers is fast becoming an established technique in the study of protein-protein interactions for both small and large scale samples. With the increased uptake of XLMS as a technique, different combinations of crosslinker types, fragmentation strategies and analysis programs are being applied to a diverse array of biological samples. This study is focused on understanding how the three variables of an XLMS experiment – crosslinker type, fragmentation and program – could generate differences in identifications, leading to increased biological coverage of a sample. Here we probe for the first time, the known enzyme:substrate interaction between yeast arginine methyltransferase Hmt1p and its substrate, heterologous nucleolar protein Npl3p. We use this known interaction as a platform to compare two analysis programs, MeroX and XlinkX2.0, using two crosslinker types, DSSO and DSBU, and two mass-spectrometry fragmentation strategies, CID/ETD and SteppedHCD. Through these we also compare different algorithm strategies, Precursor and Reporter-Ion, as well as assess the impact of restricting data searches to lysine only crosslinks versus the inclusion of serine, threonine and tyrosine as reactive residues. From this analysis we show direct evidence of Hmt1p in contact with its known methylation sites on Npl3p, the intrinsically disordered “SRGG” region. We also show through our multi-crosslinker, multi-fragmentation and multi-software approach that two approaches leads to greater understanding and depth of a crosslinked sample, and this is due to some combinations of experimental variables creating greater coverage of crosslinks than others.\n\n[METHODS]\nRecombinant protein production –Strain and culturing information was as per 24. Briefly, ORFs encoding N-terminally tagged 6xHis Hmt1p and 6xHis Npl3p were cloned into plasmid pet15b, and heterologous expression was achieved by culturing transformed E. coli to OD 600nm of 0.5, and inducing with 0.5 mM IPTG. Recombinant Hmt1p and Npl3p, were then purified on a Ni2+ charged Bio-Scale-Mini Profinity cartridge, attached to a Bio-Rad Profinia purification system according to manufacturer’s instructions.   In-vitro crosslinking with DSSO or DSBU – Equimolar (~5 μM) amounts of 6xHis-tagged Npl3p and Hmt1p were incubated for 1h at 25 °C in 20mM HEPES, 150mM NaCl, 10mM DTT, 50uM AdoMet and 1mM of either DSSO or DSBU. Following incubation, crosslinked proteins were reduced, alkylated and digested with Trypsin (Promega) as per 14. Tryptic peptide solutions were then desalted using C18 Sep-Pak (Waters) cartridges according to 14, elutions were dried by SpeedVac, and resuspended in 0.1% (v/v) formic acid.   LC-MS/MS analysis of peptides - Samples containing crosslinked Npl3p/HMT1p peptides were analysed in technical duplicate using an Ultimate 3000 UHPLC System 25 (Thermo Fischer Scientific) coupled to either a Tribrid Fusion Lumos or a Q Exactive Plus. Liquid chromatography conditions for both instruments were as follows: Solvent A (0.1% v/v formic acid in 2% v/v CH3CN), Solvent B 0.1% (v/v) formic acid in 45% (v/v) CH3CN. Gradients were 0-4min 0.1% TFA wash, 4-160 min linear gradient to 45% Solvent B, 160-164 min, linear gradient to 80% Solvent B, 164-165 min 80% Solvent B, 165-179 min linear gradient to 2% Solvent B. Peptides were ionized using nanospray ionization in positive ion mode (2 kV) for both instruments.   The Tribrid Fusion Lumos was used for MS2 CID+ETD fragmentation with parameters for CID+ETD analysis adapted from 13. Precursor ion scans were performed using Orbitrap acquisition at 60,000 FWHM, m/z range 375 to 1575. Automatic Gain Control target was set to 4×105 with a maximum inject time of 50 ms. Monoisotopic precursor selection was placed in peptide mode with dynamic exclusion set to: n times =1, exclusion duration 18 s, ± 5ppm. Ions with charge states >2 were isolated in the quadrupole and fragmented using both collision induced dissociation (CID) and electron-transfer dissociation (ETD). Data-dependent product scans using CID were measured in the Orbitrap at 15,000 FWHM using a normalised collision energy of 25 ± 5% HCD. Automatic Gain Control target 5.0e4, with parallelization on. Max inject time: 120 ms. Data-dependent ETD product scans using ETD were performed using an ETD reagent target of 2×105 with calibrated Charge-Dependent ETD parameters on.   Experiments utilizing Stepped HCD were performed on a Q Exactive Plus. Precursor ion scans were performed using 70,000 FWHM, Automatic Gain Control target 1×106 for a maximum inject time of 50 ms. Filters included, dynamic exclusion, default charge state of 3-10, m/z range 375-1575, and intensity threshold 1.2×105. The top 10 peaks were then selected for MS2 Stepped HCD. Fragment ion spectrum were acquired using 17,500 FWHM, AGC target 1e5, maximum inject time 125 ms and an isolation window 2.5 m/z. HCD normalised collision energies were 25, 30, 35.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
